======================================================================
DIVERGENCE ANALYSIS
======================================================================

  P(2)=2, T(3)=2. MATCH.
  P(3)=4, T(4)=4. MATCH.
  P(4)=12, T(5)=12. MATCH.
  P(5)=48, T(6)=56. DIVERGE!
  P(6)=296, T(7)=456. DIVERGE!

  FIRST DIVERGENCE at n=5: P(5) = 48, T(6) = 56.
  GAP = T(6) - P(5) = 8
  T(6) is LARGER: there are 8 more classes at n=6 than perspectives at n=5.

  THE BIJECTION THAT WORKS AT SMALL n:
    Given a pointed tournament (T, v) on n vertices:
    Remove v to get a tournament T' on n-1 vertices.
    Equivalently: adding a new vertex to T' and connecting it
    in a specific way gives T back with v as the new vertex.

    At n=3→4: each pointed 3-tournament (4 of them) corresponds
    to exactly one 4-tournament (4 of them). BIJECTION.

    At n=5→6: 48 pointed 5-tournaments but 56 unpointed 6-tournaments.
    So 56 - 48 = 8 tournaments on 6 vertices are NOT reachable
    by 'adding a vertex' to a pointed 5-tournament.
    (Or: some 6-tournaments arise from MULTIPLE pointed 5-tournaments.)

